<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Julius II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1443">1443</a>–<a href="/years/1513">1513</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Giuliano della Rovere · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Julius II, born Giuliano della Rovere, was pope from 1503 to 1513 and known as the 'warrior pope'. He reclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a>'s fall, personally led campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1506 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> in 1511, and gave Urbino and Pesaro to his nephew. He commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling</a> and began the new St Peter's Basilica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii.jpg" alt="Pope Julius II" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raphael · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Julius_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giuliano della Rovere was born at Albisola near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a>, in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, into a noble but impoverished family. He was educated by his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Francesco della Rovere</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and studied at Perugia. When his uncle was elected Pope Sixtus IV in 1471, Giuliano became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpentras">Carpentras</a> and, in an act of open nepotism, a cardinal within two months. He held several bishoprics at once and from 1475 was the first Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. He was a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Rodrigo Borgia</a>, who won the conclave of 1492 as Alexander VI, and in 1494 della Rovere took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and urged Charles VIII to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-rovere">Della Rovere</a> was elected on 1 November 1503, in a few hours, after winning over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> with promises of money and continued support in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>. Historians such as Ludwig von Pastor say that bribery and promises played a part. He took the name Julius, used by only one earlier pope. Early in his reign he worked to end the power of the Borgia family and forbade anyone to speak or think of Alexander VI. He aimed to centralise the Papal States and to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the barbarians.</p><p>Julius led troops in person on at least two occasions: against Giovanni Bentivoglio at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> in 1506–07 and against the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> in 1510–12. In 1508 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII of France</a>, Emperor Maximilian I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-cambrai">League of Cambrai</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which he placed under an interdict in 1509. He convened the Fifth Council of the Lateran, which met from May 1512, after Louis XII and Maximilian had backed a rival council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> to threaten him. In 1506 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-museums">Vatican Museums</a> and organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontifical-swiss-guard">Swiss Guard</a> for his protection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Julius was seriously ill from 1512, and on 4 February 1513 he discussed his funeral arrangements; he died on 21 February. He was one of the most powerful popes and a central figure of the High Renaissance, and under him the Papal States gained power and centralisation. He revived the plan to replace the old basilica of St Peter's, though Giuliano da Sangallo's proposal was not accepted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> satirised him after his death in Julius Excluded from Heaven, which shows him drunk and refused entry by St Peter. His character was violent and gruff, and his Master of Ceremonies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> de Grassis, said he hardly ever jested.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Julius II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parma and Piacenza, two prosperous cities of the Po plain on the Via Emilia, had belonged to the Duchy of Milan for much of the late Middle Ages before <b>Pope Julius II</b> annexed them to the Papal States in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Julius II</b>, who founded the Swiss Guard in 1506, personally led his army in winter 1511 and entered the captured town of Mirandola through a breach in its walls.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>For six months in 1504 a diagonal crack in the vault made the chapel unusable, and <b>Pope Julius II</b> (Giuliano della Rovere) had the damaged painting removed by Piero Roselli, a friend of Michelangelo.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Julius II</b> laid the first stone of Bramante's new basilica in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Julius II</b>, Louis XII of France, Emperor Maximilian I and Ferdinand of Aragon formally concluded the League of Cambrai against Venice.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1511 <b>Pope Julius II</b> personally led the siege of the fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mirandola">Duchy of Mirandola</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Albisola Superiore, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.34,8.51&amp;year=1488" aria-label="Albisola Superiore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pope Julius II"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5519 3767h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5519 3767h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.34,8.51&amp;year=1488">What was here in 1488? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
